+
+## Start-of-task protocol (every task, including small ones)
+
+Do this **before your first edit**. It is cheap, and skipping it is the failure this section exists to prevent.
+
+1. **Skill scan.** Compare the task with the skill list in your context. `read` the `SKILL.md` of every skill
+   whose description matches (`dev-workflows` matches any bug fix, feature, refactor or codebase exploration;
+   `pi-lens-ast-grep` matches searching or renaming code patterns; `subagents` matches any request to delegate).
+   A skill only counts if you actually `read` its `SKILL.md` — naming it is not enough. Skills live under
+   `~/.pi/agent/`, outside your working directory — reading them is always allowed.
+   Then, before your first edit, record it: `note` (section `workflow`) → `Skills read: <names, or "none matched">`.
+2. **Delegation check.** Dispatch a subagent (the `subagent` tool, or `run_dev_workflow`) instead of working inline
+   when **any** of these holds:
+   - you do not already know which files to change → `scout` first (or `run_dev_workflow` type `explore`);
+   - you expect to touch more than one source file, or a bug's cause is still unclear after ~6 tool calls →
+     `run_dev_workflow` (`bugfix` for a bug, `swat` for a feature);
+   - you are about to declare done after editing source code → `reviewer` on your diff (Definition of done);
+   - the same problem has failed verification twice → checkpoint & fork (next section).
+3. **Inline is allowed only when none of those hold.** Say which triggers you checked in the same `note`.
+
+Task wording such as "autonomous", "no human interaction" or "minimal changes" does **not** waive this — it means
+*don't ask the user questions* and *keep the diff small*. A harness warning to "stop investigating" means stop
+investigating **yourself**: dispatching a `scout` is the sanctioned way to keep going. An explicit instruction not
+to delegate (e.g. "don't use subagents") still wins.
+
+**Subagents:** if your first message begins with `Task:` you are a subagent — do step 1 only and never dispatch
+further subagents.
+
+## When a fix keeps failing (checkpoint & fork)
+
+After **2 consecutive failed verifications of the same problem** (same test or error), stop iterating in place —
+your context now carries dead ends and stale assumptions.
+
+1. **Checkpoint.** `note` (sections `errors` / `learnings` / `worklog`): the problem, each approach tried and *why it
+   failed*, ranked hypotheses, and the current diff/state.
+2. **Fork.** Dispatch a fresh subagent (`subagent` tool: `planner` if a new approach needs designing, otherwise
+   `worker` or `general`) with **only** the checkpoint and the repro steps — not the conversation.
+3. **Merge or escalate.** If the fork verifies, take its result. If it also fails, record it with `learn`, then ask
+   the user (`question`) if one is attached; otherwise stop and report what you know instead of a third blind attempt.
 
